To view the animations of a .glb (GL Transmission Format) 3D object in a web browser, you will need to open the file using a compatible web glTF viewer that supports animations, such as Three.js or A-Frame. Here's how to find out the names of the animations:

1. First, make sure you have access to the .glb file.

2. Open the file in a web glTF viewer, such as ThreeJS or A-Frame.

3. Find the options to view the scene hierarchy, which shows the structure of the 3D object.

4. Look for a section related to animations, may be under the model's Scene or Animation node. The name of the animations may be displayed here.

5. If you can't find the animation details in the viewer, try downloading and opening the .glb file in a glTF viewer on your computer, such as Blender, to inspect the scene and animation data.

6. If more details are needed, you can examine the content of the .glb file in a text editor or use a glTF schema validator, like https://gltf-validator.com/ or https://editor.glTF.ORG/, to inspect the animation channels, animations, and skins in the JSON portion of the GLTF.

The glTF schema defines a hierarchy for animations that you might find useful:

- `animations`: an array of animation objects, usually each representing a separate animation channel. The order of the objects in this array is dependent on the GLTF parser used.
- `animation`: a single animation object that corresponds to a blend shape or skeleton animation. This is an alternative to the animations array.
- `nodes`: an array of nodes that make up the scene hierarchy. Each node has a `name` property that provides a unique identifier for the node.
- `skin`: an optional skin object that contains references to mesh and skeleton data.

Once you have located the animation nodes, you can review the animation channels and parameters, such as the targets (nodes) and tracks (keyframe animations), to identify the animation names.

Note: If the .glb file doesn't contain any animations, it will not display any animation-related information in the scene hierarchy or Animation node, so you won't need to look for animation names in that case. Instead, you may find information regarding morph targets or animations in separate JSON or binary files that accompany the .glb file.asing with variables.﻿# create a random quote generator using javascript that pulls quotes from an api or local file, includes an author if available, and changes the background color with each quote generation. Make the UI visually appealing with fonts, spacing, and button styling. Add functionality to generate a new quote when the user clicks a "generate quote" button, and allow users to tweet the quote to their social media account. Use responsive design principles to ensure the app displays correctly on various screen sizes.﻿# Solution

